// Class 12 Accountancy practice bank, part 1: Partnership Accounts (chapters 1-6).
StudyData.addPractice(12, "accountancy", {
  "partnership-fundamentals": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The minimum number of partners required to form a partnership firm is:", options: ["One", "Two", "Three", "Seven"], answer: 1, explain: "A partnership is a relation between two or more persons, so the minimum is **two**. (Seven is the minimum for a public company.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "Under the fixed capital method, the partners' drawings are debited to the partners':", options: ["Capital A/c", "Partner's Loan A/c", "Profit and Loss A/c", "Current A/c"], answer: 3, explain: "Under the fixed capital method the capital account changes only for additional capital or permanent withdrawal. Drawings, interest, salary and share of profit are recorded in the **current account**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is an appropriation of profit?", options: ["Partner's salary as per the deed", "Interest on partner's loan", "Rent paid to a partner", "Commission to an employee"], answer: 0, explain: "A partner's salary allowed by the deed is an appropriation, debited to the Profit and Loss Appropriation A/c. Interest on a partner's loan, rent to a partner and an employee's commission are charges against profit.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When the deed is silent, a partner lent ₹50,000 to the firm for the whole year. The interest allowed to him is:", options: ["Nil", "₹5,000", "₹3,000", "₹6,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Section 13 allows interest on a partner's loan at 6% p.a.: 50,000 × 6/100 = **₹3,000**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a feature of a partnership firm?", options: ["Perpetual succession", "Mutual agency", "Unlimited liability", "Agreement to share profits"], answer: 0, explain: "Perpetual succession is a feature of a company. A partnership has an agreement, mutual agency and unlimited liability, and it is affected by the death or retirement of a partner.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A partner's capital on 1 April 2024 was ₹3,00,000. On 1 October 2024 he introduced additional capital of ₹60,000. If interest on capital is allowed at 8% p.a., the interest for the year ended 31 March 2025 is:", options: ["₹28,800", "₹26,400", "₹24,000", "₹27,600"], answer: 1, explain: "Interest on ₹3,00,000 for 12 months = ₹24,000. Interest on ₹60,000 for 6 months = 60,000 × 8/100 × 6/12 = ₹2,400. Total = **₹26,400**. (₹28,800 wrongly charges the additional capital for the full year.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A partner withdrew ₹10,000 at the beginning of every quarter during the year. Interest on drawings is charged at 12% p.a. The interest on drawings is:", options: ["₹2,400", "₹1,800", "₹4,800", "₹3,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Total drawings = ₹40,000. For quarterly withdrawals at the beginning of each quarter the average period is 7.5 months. Interest = 40,000 × 12/100 × 7.5/12 = **₹3,000**. (6 months gives ₹2,400; 4.5 months gives ₹1,800.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A and B are entitled to interest on capital of ₹18,000 and ₹12,000. The net profit of the year is only ₹20,000. The deed treats interest on capital as an appropriation. The amount credited to A as interest on capital is:", options: ["₹18,000", "₹12,000", "₹10,000", "₹8,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Interest is allowed only out of profit. As profit (₹20,000) is less than the interest due (₹30,000), profit is divided in the ratio of the interest, 18:12 = 3:2. A gets 20,000 × 3/5 = **₹12,000** (and B gets ₹8,000).", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Interest on a partner's loan is payable to him even if the firm earns a loss.\nReason (R): Interest on a partner's loan is a charge against profit and not an appropriation of profit.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "A charge is debited to the Profit and Loss A/c whether the firm earns a profit or a loss, so the loan interest must be paid in either case. Hence R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Ravi and Sunil run a firm without any written deed. Ravi has put in ₹5,00,000 and Sunil ₹2,00,000 as capital. The profit for the year is ₹84,000 and Ravi claims a bigger share because of his larger capital.\nAs per the Partnership Act, Sunil's share of the profit is:", options: ["₹24,000", "₹28,000", "₹42,000", "₹60,000"], answer: 2, explain: "When the deed is silent, profits are shared equally and no interest on capital is allowed. Sunil gets 84,000 ÷ 2 = **₹42,000**. (₹24,000 would be a share in the ratio of capitals, 2/7.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits 5:3:2. The firm guarantees Z a minimum profit share of ₹60,000. The profit for the year is ₹2,40,000 and the deficiency is borne by X and Y in their profit sharing ratio. X finally receives:", options: ["₹1,12,500", "₹1,14,000", "₹1,08,000", "₹1,20,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Z's share = 2/10 × 2,40,000 = ₹48,000. Deficiency = 60,000 − 48,000 = ₹12,000, borne by X and Y in 5:3: X ₹7,500, Y ₹4,500. X's share = 1,20,000 − 7,500 = **₹1,12,500**. Check: 1,12,500 + 67,500 + 60,000 = 2,40,000.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2 and have fixed capitals of ₹4,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. The deed provides interest on capital at 10% p.a., but it was omitted from last year's accounts. The correcting entry is:", options: ["A's Current A/c Dr. ₹4,000 To B's Current A/c ₹4,000", "B's Current A/c Dr. ₹24,000 To A's Current A/c ₹24,000", "B's Current A/c Dr. ₹4,000 To A's Current A/c ₹4,000", "A's Current A/c Dr. ₹36,000 To B's Current A/c ₹36,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Interest due: A ₹40,000, B ₹20,000 (total ₹60,000). This total should have been borne by A and B in 3:2: A ₹36,000, B ₹24,000. Net: A should get 40,000 − 36,000 = ₹4,000 more; B should get 20,000 − 24,000 = ₹4,000 less. So B's Current A/c is debited and A's Current A/c credited by **₹4,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: Priya lent ₹1,00,000 to the firm on 1 July 2024. The deed is silent on interest on loan. For the year ended 31 March 2025, the firm's profit before charging this interest was ₹3,000.\nAfter charging the interest on Priya's loan, the Profit and Loss A/c shows:", options: ["Profit of ₹3,000", "Loss of ₹1,500", "Profit of ₹1,500", "Loss of ₹4,500"], answer: 1, explain: "The interest is allowed at 6% p.a. for 9 months: 1,00,000 × 6/100 × 9/12 = ₹4,500. It is a charge against profit, so the result is 3,000 − 4,500 = **loss of ₹1,500**.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Interest on capital is always a charge against the profit of the firm.\nReason (R): Interest on capital can be allowed to the partners only if the firm has sufficient profit, when it is treated as an appropriation.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: interest on capital is an appropriation of profit unless the deed specifically states that it is a charge. R is true: as an appropriation it is allowed only out of profits (if profit is insufficient, profit is divided in the ratio of the interest; in case of loss nothing is allowed).", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Why is the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account called an extension of the Profit and Loss Account?", a: "Because it starts from the net profit shown by the Profit and Loss A/c and shows how that profit is distributed (appropriated) among the partners.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Under which capital method does the capital account of a partner always show a credit balance? Give a reason.", a: "The **fixed capital method**. The capital account changes only for additional capital introduced or capital permanently withdrawn, and all other items (interest, salary, drawings, profit) go to the current account.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Gupta withdrew ₹4,000 at the end of every month during the year. Interest on drawings is charged at 9% p.a. Calculate the interest on drawings.", a: "Total drawings = 4,000 × 12 = ₹48,000.\nFor withdrawals at the end of every month, average period = (11 + 0) ÷ 2 = 5.5 months.\nInterest on drawings = 48,000 × 9/100 × 5.5/12 = **₹1,980**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In the absence of a partnership deed, Bhatia lent ₹2,00,000 to the firm on 1 October 2024. Calculate the interest payable to him for the year ended 31 March 2025 and state where it is debited.", a: "Interest on a partner's loan is allowed at 6% p.a. even when the deed is silent.\nPeriod = 1 October 2024 to 31 March 2025 = 6 months.\nInterest = 2,00,000 × 6/100 × 6/12 = **₹6,000**.\nIt is a charge against profit, so it is debited to the **Profit and Loss A/c** (not the Appropriation A/c).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Anil and Bimal share profits 2:1 and have fixed capitals of ₹5,00,000 and ₹3,00,000. The deed provides interest on capital at 6% p.a., but it was omitted while preparing last year's accounts. Pass the adjusting entry.", a: "Interest due: Anil = 5,00,000 × 6% = ₹30,000; Bimal = 3,00,000 × 6% = ₹18,000. Total ₹48,000.\nSince the interest was not given, this ₹48,000 of profit was shared 2:1: Anil got ₹32,000 and Bimal got ₹16,000.\nAnil should have got ₹30,000, so he got ₹2,000 too much. Bimal should have got ₹18,000, so he got ₹2,000 too little.\nEntry:\nAnil's Current A/c Dr. ₹2,000\n  To Bimal's Current A/c ₹2,000\n(Being interest on capital omitted last year adjusted)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 2:2:1. C is guaranteed a minimum profit share of ₹30,000 by A. The profit for the year is ₹1,20,000. Show how the profit is distributed.", a: "C's share = 1/5 × 1,20,000 = ₹24,000, which is less than the guarantee of ₹30,000.\nDeficiency = 30,000 − 24,000 = ₹6,000, borne entirely by A (the guarantor).\nA's share = 2/5 × 1,20,000 = ₹48,000 − 6,000 = **₹42,000**.\nB's share = 2/5 × 1,20,000 = **₹48,000**.\nC receives **₹30,000**.\nCheck: 42,000 + 48,000 + 30,000 = ₹1,20,000.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "B's capital account (fluctuating capital) shows a closing balance of ₹3,80,000. During the year B drew ₹40,000 and introduced additional capital of ₹30,000. His credits for the year were interest on capital ₹20,000, salary ₹12,000 and share of profit ₹70,000. Interest charged on his drawings was ₹2,000. Find his opening capital.", a: "Capital A/c of B:\nDr side: Drawings ₹40,000; Interest on drawings ₹2,000; Balance c/d ₹3,80,000 (total ₹4,22,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d (opening capital) ₹2,90,000 (balancing figure); Additional capital ₹30,000; Interest on capital ₹20,000; Salary ₹12,000; Share of profit ₹70,000 (total ₹4,22,000).\nOpening capital = 4,22,000 − (30,000 + 20,000 + 12,000 + 70,000) = **₹2,90,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "X and Y share profits 3:2. Their fixed capitals are ₹5,00,000 and ₹3,00,000. The deed provides: interest on capital at 8% p.a.; interest on drawings at 6% p.a.; a salary of ₹30,000 per year to Y. The net profit for the year was ₹1,60,000. X drew ₹5,000 at the beginning of every month. Y drew ₹20,000 on 1 July 2024 and ₹20,000 on 1 January 2025. The year ends on 31 March 2025. The opening current accounts were: X ₹10,000 (Cr.), Y ₹4,000 (Dr.). Prepare the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account and the partners' current accounts.", a: "Working:\nInterest on capital: X = 5,00,000 × 8% = ₹40,000; Y = 3,00,000 × 8% = ₹24,000.\nInterest on X's drawings: total ₹60,000 for 6.5 months = 60,000 × 6/100 × 6.5/12 = ₹1,950.\nInterest on Y's drawings (product method): 20,000 × 9 = 1,80,000; 20,000 × 3 = 60,000; total 2,40,000. Interest = 2,40,000 × 6/100 × 1/12 = ₹1,200.\n\nProfit and Loss Appropriation A/c:\nDr side: Interest on capital: X ₹40,000, Y ₹24,000; Y's salary ₹30,000; Profit transferred: X ₹41,490, Y ₹27,660 (total ₹1,63,150) | Cr side: Net profit ₹1,60,000; Interest on drawings: X ₹1,950, Y ₹1,200 (total ₹1,63,150).\n(Divisible profit = 1,63,150 − 94,000 = ₹69,150; X 3/5 = ₹41,490, Y 2/5 = ₹27,660.)\n\nX's Current A/c:\nDr side: Drawings ₹60,000; Interest on drawings ₹1,950; Balance c/d ₹29,540 (total ₹91,490) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹10,000; Interest on capital ₹40,000; Share of profit ₹41,490 (total ₹91,490).\n\nY's Current A/c:\nDr side: Balance b/d ₹4,000; Drawings ₹40,000; Interest on drawings ₹1,200; Balance c/d ₹36,460 (total ₹81,660) | Cr side: Interest on capital ₹24,000; Salary ₹30,000; Share of profit ₹27,660 (total ₹81,660).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2. C is guaranteed by the firm that his share of profit (excluding interest on capital) will not be less than ₹40,000. Interest on capital allowed is A ₹20,000, B ₹12,000 and C ₹8,000. The net profit for the year is ₹2,00,000. Prepare the Profit and Loss Appropriation Account.", a: "Profit after interest on capital = 2,00,000 − 40,000 = ₹1,60,000.\nShares: A = 5/10 × 1,60,000 = ₹80,000; B = 3/10 × 1,60,000 = ₹48,000; C = 2/10 × 1,60,000 = ₹32,000.\nC's share is less than ₹40,000, so the deficiency = 40,000 − 32,000 = ₹8,000.\nThe firm gives the guarantee, so A and B bear it in their profit sharing ratio 5:3: A ₹5,000, B ₹3,000.\nFinal shares: A = 80,000 − 5,000 = ₹75,000; B = 48,000 − 3,000 = ₹45,000; C = ₹40,000.\n\nProfit and Loss Appropriation A/c:\nDr side: Interest on capital: A ₹20,000, B ₹12,000, C ₹8,000; Share of profit: A ₹75,000, B ₹45,000, C ₹40,000 (total ₹2,00,000) | Cr side: Net profit ₹2,00,000 (total ₹2,00,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Neha and Ritu are partners. Their deed provides interest on capital at 10% p.a. and interest on drawings at 12% p.a. Neha's capital on 1 April 2024 was ₹6,00,000 and she introduced a further ₹1,20,000 on 1 October 2024. Ritu's capital was ₹4,00,000 throughout. Ritu withdrew ₹3,000 at the end of every month. On 1 January 2025 Ritu lent ₹50,000 to the firm; the deed is silent on interest on partners' loans. The year ends on 31 March 2025.\n(i) Calculate Neha's interest on capital.\n(ii) Calculate the interest on Ritu's drawings.\n(iii) Calculate the interest on Ritu's loan and state where it is debited.", a: "(i) Interest on ₹6,00,000 for 12 months = ₹60,000. Interest on ₹1,20,000 for 6 months = 1,20,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = ₹6,000. Total = **₹66,000**.\n(ii) Total drawings = 3,000 × 12 = ₹36,000. End of each month: average period 5.5 months. Interest = 36,000 × 12/100 × 5.5/12 = **₹1,980**.\n(iii) Interest on loan at 6% p.a. (Partnership Act) for 3 months = 50,000 × 6/100 × 3/12 = **₹750**. It is a charge and is debited to the Profit and Loss A/c.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "goodwill-nature-and-valuation": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Goodwill is best classified as:", options: ["A fictitious asset", "A current asset", "An intangible asset", "A tangible fixed asset"], answer: 2, explain: "Goodwill has value but cannot be seen or touched, and it cannot be separated from the business. It is an **intangible asset**; it is not fictitious because it can be sold along with the business.", level: 1 },
      { q: "As per AS 26, goodwill is recorded in the books of a firm only when:", options: ["It is self-generated", "It is purchased, i.e. a consideration is paid for it", "The firm earns super profits", "All the partners agree to record it"], answer: 1, explain: "AS 26 permits only **purchased goodwill** to be recorded. Self-generated goodwill is never recorded.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Super profit is equal to:", options: ["Average profit + Normal profit", "Normal profit − Average profit", "Average profit × Normal rate of return", "Average profit − Normal profit"], answer: 3, explain: "Super profit is the excess of average profit over the normal profit expected on the capital employed: **Average profit − Normal profit**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a method of valuing goodwill in a partnership firm?", options: ["Replacement cost method", "Average profit method", "Super profit method", "Capitalisation method"], answer: 0, explain: "Goodwill is valued by the average profit, super profit and capitalisation methods. Replacement cost is not a goodwill valuation method.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The weighted average profit method of valuing goodwill is preferred when:", options: ["Profits are the same every year", "Profits show a rising or a falling trend", "The firm has made losses throughout", "Goodwill already appears in the books"], answer: 1, explain: "When profits show a trend, recent years are more reliable, so they are given higher weights (1, 2, 3 …).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Profits of three years were ₹60,000, ₹70,000 and ₹45,000. The second year's profit includes an abnormal gain of ₹10,000 and the third year's profit is after an abnormal loss of ₹15,000. Goodwill at 3 years' purchase of average profit is:", options: ["₹1,75,000", "₹1,70,000", "₹1,80,000", "₹2,10,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Adjusted profits: 60,000; 70,000 − 10,000 = 60,000; 45,000 + 15,000 = 60,000. Average profit = ₹60,000. Goodwill = 60,000 × 3 = **₹1,80,000**. (₹1,75,000 uses unadjusted profits.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A firm's average profit is ₹1,20,000, its capital employed is ₹8,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%. Goodwill at 4 years' purchase of super profit is:", options: ["₹4,80,000", "₹40,000", "₹4,00,000", "₹1,60,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Normal profit = 8,00,000 × 10% = ₹80,000. Super profit = 1,20,000 − 80,000 = ₹40,000. Goodwill = 40,000 × 4 = **₹1,60,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Profits of the last three years were ₹30,000, ₹36,000 and ₹48,000. Using weights 1, 2 and 3 respectively, the weighted average profit is:", options: ["₹41,000", "₹38,000", "₹43,000", "₹36,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Weighted profit = 30,000 × 1 + 36,000 × 2 + 48,000 × 3 = 30,000 + 72,000 + 1,44,000 = ₹2,46,000. Sum of weights = 6. Weighted average = 2,46,000 ÷ 6 = **₹41,000**. (₹38,000 is the simple average.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Self-generated goodwill is not recorded in the books of a firm.\nReason (R): AS 26 permits only purchased goodwill to be shown as an asset, because its cost can be measured reliably.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Self-generated goodwill has no objectively measurable cost, so AS 26 does not allow it to be recorded; only goodwill bought for a price is recorded. R correctly explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Sharma and Sons is a sweet shop with capital employed of ₹10,00,000. Similar shops in the area earn a normal return of 12% on capital. The shop's average profit for the last three years is ₹1,50,000.\nThe super profit of the shop is:", options: ["₹1,20,000", "₹30,000", "₹1,50,000", "₹2,50,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Normal profit = 10,00,000 × 12% = ₹1,20,000. Super profit = 1,50,000 − 1,20,000 = **₹30,000**. The shop earns more than similar shops, so it has goodwill.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The assets of a firm total ₹9,50,000, including goodwill ₹50,000 and a debit balance of Profit and Loss A/c ₹20,000. Outside liabilities are ₹3,00,000. Average profit is ₹90,000 and the normal rate of return is 12%. Goodwill by the capitalisation of average profit method is:", options: ["₹1,50,000", "₹1,00,000", "₹1,70,000", "₹2,50,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Capital employed = 9,50,000 − 50,000 (goodwill) − 20,000 (fictitious asset) − 3,00,000 = ₹5,80,000. Capitalised value = 90,000 × 100/12 = ₹7,50,000. Goodwill = 7,50,000 − 5,80,000 = **₹1,70,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The average profit of a firm is ₹1,50,000, but from next year an additional rent of ₹20,000 a year will be payable. Capital employed is ₹6,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 12%. Goodwill at 3 years' purchase of super profit is:", options: ["₹2,34,000", "₹58,000", "₹1,74,000", "₹2,94,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Expected average profit = 1,50,000 − 20,000 = ₹1,30,000. Normal profit = 6,00,000 × 12% = ₹72,000. Super profit = 58,000. Goodwill = 58,000 × 3 = **₹1,74,000**. (₹2,34,000 ignores the extra rent.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: Tara and Uma plan to sell their firm. The buyer offers to pay goodwill equal to the capitalised value of the super profit. Average profit is ₹60,000, capital employed is ₹4,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%.\nThe goodwill offered is:", options: ["₹6,00,000", "₹20,000", "₹60,000", "₹2,00,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Normal profit = 4,00,000 × 10% = ₹40,000. Super profit = 60,000 − 40,000 = ₹20,000. Capitalised super profit = 20,000 × 100/10 = **₹2,00,000**. Check: capitalised average profit 6,00,000 − capital employed 4,00,000 = ₹2,00,000.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The weighted average method gives more importance to the profits of recent years.\nReason (R): In this method, the largest weight is given to the earliest year.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: weights such as 1, 2, 3 are given in increasing order, so recent years carry more weight. R is false: the smallest weight goes to the earliest year and the largest to the latest year.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define goodwill.", a: "Goodwill is the value of the reputation of a firm in respect of the profits expected in future over and above the normal profits. It is an intangible asset.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the treatment of self-generated goodwill under AS 26.", a: "Under AS 26 (Intangible Assets), self-generated goodwill is not recorded in the books; only purchased goodwill is recorded.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between purchased goodwill and self-generated goodwill.", a: "(1) Purchased goodwill is acquired by paying a price, for example when a business is bought; self-generated goodwill is built up over time by the firm's own reputation and efficiency.\n(2) Purchased goodwill is recorded in the books at the price paid (AS 26); self-generated goodwill is not recorded because its cost cannot be measured reliably.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The profits of a firm for the last four years were: 2021 ₹80,000; 2022 ₹90,000 (including an abnormal gain of ₹10,000); 2023 ₹70,000 (after an abnormal loss of ₹5,000); 2024 ₹1,15,000. Calculate goodwill at 2 years' purchase of the average profit.", a: "Adjusted profits: 2021 ₹80,000; 2022 90,000 − 10,000 = ₹80,000; 2023 70,000 + 5,000 = ₹75,000; 2024 ₹1,15,000.\nTotal = ₹3,50,000; Average profit = 3,50,000 ÷ 4 = ₹87,500.\nGoodwill = 87,500 × 2 = **₹1,75,000**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The total assets of a firm are ₹10,00,000, including goodwill of ₹1,00,000. Outside liabilities are ₹3,00,000. The average profit is ₹90,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%. Calculate goodwill by (a) 3 years' purchase of super profit and (b) capitalisation of super profit.", a: "Capital employed = 10,00,000 − 1,00,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹6,00,000.\nNormal profit = 6,00,000 × 10% = ₹60,000. Super profit = 90,000 − 60,000 = ₹30,000.\n(a) Goodwill = 30,000 × 3 = **₹90,000**.\n(b) Goodwill = 30,000 × 100/10 = **₹3,00,000**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The profits of a firm for the last three years were ₹42,000, ₹48,000 and ₹60,000. Calculate goodwill at 3 years' purchase of the weighted average profit, giving weights 1, 2 and 3 to the three years. Why is this method suitable here?", a: "Weighted profit = 42,000 × 1 + 48,000 × 2 + 60,000 × 3 = 42,000 + 96,000 + 1,80,000 = ₹3,18,000.\nSum of weights = 6. Weighted average profit = 3,18,000 ÷ 6 = ₹53,000.\nGoodwill = 53,000 × 3 = **₹1,59,000**.\nThe method suits this firm because profits are rising every year, so recent profits should count for more than old ones.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain any three factors that affect the value of goodwill of a firm.", a: "(1) **Nature of business**: a stable business with steady demand earns higher profits and more goodwill.\n(2) **Location**: a shop in a central, easily accessible place attracts more customers.\n(3) **Efficiency of management**: efficient management gives higher productivity and lower costs, hence higher profits.\n(Others: market situation, special advantages such as patents or licences.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The profits of a firm for the last five years were: 2020 ₹1,20,000; 2021 ₹1,40,000; 2022 ₹80,000 (after a loss by fire of ₹30,000 not covered by insurance); 2023 ₹1,50,000 (including a profit of ₹20,000 on the sale of an old machine); 2024 ₹1,40,000. From next year a manager's salary of ₹18,000 a year will have to be paid, which was not charged earlier. Calculate (a) goodwill at 3 years' purchase of the adjusted average profit and (b) goodwill at 4 years' purchase of super profit, if capital employed is ₹8,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%.", a: "Adjusted profits: 2020 ₹1,20,000; 2021 ₹1,40,000; 2022 80,000 + 30,000 = ₹1,10,000; 2023 1,50,000 − 20,000 = ₹1,30,000; 2024 ₹1,40,000.\nTotal = ₹6,40,000; average = 6,40,000 ÷ 5 = ₹1,28,000.\nLess manager's salary ₹18,000: adjusted average profit = ₹1,10,000.\n(a) Goodwill = 1,10,000 × 3 = **₹3,30,000**.\n(b) Normal profit = 8,00,000 × 10% = ₹80,000. Super profit = 1,10,000 − 80,000 = ₹30,000. Goodwill = 30,000 × 4 = **₹1,20,000**.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "The balance sheet of a firm shows: Land ₹6,00,000; Plant ₹4,00,000; Stock ₹2,00,000; Debtors ₹1,60,000; Cash ₹40,000; Goodwill ₹60,000. Its outside liabilities are creditors ₹1,20,000 and a bank loan ₹2,40,000. The average profit is ₹1,56,000 and the normal rate of return is 12%. Find goodwill by (a) capitalisation of average profit and (b) capitalisation of super profit. Show that both give the same result.", a: "Total assets = 6,00,000 + 4,00,000 + 2,00,000 + 1,60,000 + 40,000 + 60,000 = ₹14,60,000.\nCapital employed = 14,60,000 − 60,000 (goodwill) − 3,60,000 (outside liabilities) = ₹10,40,000.\n(a) Capitalised value = 1,56,000 × 100/12 = ₹13,00,000. Goodwill = 13,00,000 − 10,40,000 = **₹2,60,000**.\n(b) Normal profit = 10,40,000 × 12% = ₹1,24,800. Super profit = 1,56,000 − 1,24,800 = ₹31,200. Goodwill = 31,200 × 100/12 = **₹2,60,000**.\nBoth methods give ₹2,60,000.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Gagan and Harpreet are partners and Harpreet is about to retire, so the goodwill of the firm has to be valued. The net profits of the last three years were ₹1,05,000, ₹1,20,000 and ₹1,35,000. The capital employed in the firm is ₹7,50,000 and the normal rate of return in the industry is 12%. Goodwill is to be valued at 4 years' purchase of super profit.\n(i) Calculate the average profit.\n(ii) Calculate the normal profit and super profit.\n(iii) Calculate the goodwill and state whether it will be recorded in the books.", a: "(i) Average profit = (1,05,000 + 1,20,000 + 1,35,000) ÷ 3 = 3,60,000 ÷ 3 = **₹1,20,000**.\n(ii) Normal profit = 7,50,000 × 12% = **₹90,000**. Super profit = 1,20,000 − 90,000 = **₹30,000**.\n(iii) Goodwill = 30,000 × 4 = **₹1,20,000**. It is self-generated goodwill, so under AS 26 it is not recorded in the books; it is only used to compensate the partners (Harpreet's share is adjusted through the partners' capital accounts).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "change-in-profit-sharing-ratio": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The sacrificing ratio is calculated as:", options: ["New ratio − Old ratio", "Old ratio − New ratio", "Old ratio ÷ New ratio", "Old ratio + New ratio"], answer: 1, explain: "Sacrificing ratio = **Old share − New share**. A positive result means the partner sacrifices; a negative result (New − Old positive) means he gains.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When the profit sharing ratio of existing partners changes, accumulated profits shown in the balance sheet are distributed among partners in:", options: ["The new ratio", "The capital ratio", "Equal ratio", "The old ratio"], answer: 3, explain: "Accumulated profits and reserves were earned in the old ratio period, so they belong to the partners in the **old ratio**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. They decide to share equally in future. Which statement is correct?", options: ["A gains 1/5", "A gains 1/10", "B gains 1/10", "Both share equally, so no one gains"], answer: 2, explain: "A: old 3/5 = 6/10, new 5/10, so A sacrifices 1/10. B: old 4/10, new 5/10, so **B gains 1/10**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A change in the profit sharing ratio among the existing partners amounts to:", options: ["Dissolution of the firm", "Reconstitution of the firm", "Retirement of a partner", "Admission of a partner"], answer: 1, explain: "Any change in the existing agreement among partners reconstitutes the firm. The old partnership ends and a new one begins, but the firm continues.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On a change in the profit sharing ratio, the sacrificing partners are compensated for goodwill by the:", options: ["Gaining partners", "The firm out of profit", "Sacrificing partners themselves", "A new partner"], answer: 0, explain: "The partners who gain a larger share of future profits pay the partners who give up a share. The entry is: Gaining Partners' Capital A/c Dr. → To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/c.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2. They decide to share profits 2:2:1 in future. Which of the following is correct?", options: ["A gains 1/10; B sacrifices 1/10; C unchanged", "A sacrifices 1/5; B gains 1/5; C unchanged", "A sacrifices 1/10; B gains 1/10; C sacrifices 1/10", "A sacrifices 1/10; B gains 1/10; C unchanged"], answer: 3, explain: "Old: 5/10, 3/10, 2/10. New: 2/5, 2/5, 1/5 = 4/10, 4/10, 2/10. A: 5/10 − 4/10 = 1/10 sacrifice. B: 3/10 − 4/10 = −1/10, a gain of 1/10. C: 2/10 − 2/10 = 0.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2 and decide to share equally. The goodwill of the firm is valued at ₹1,50,000. The amount B pays to A through the capital accounts is:", options: ["₹30,000", "₹60,000", "₹15,000", "₹75,000"], answer: 2, explain: "A sacrifices 3/5 − 1/2 = 1/10. B pays 1,50,000 × 1/10 = **₹15,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2 and decide to share equally. A General Reserve of ₹60,000 appears in the balance sheet and is to be distributed. A is credited with:", options: ["₹30,000", "₹36,000", "₹24,000", "₹60,000"], answer: 1, explain: "The reserve is distributed in the old ratio 3:2. A gets 60,000 × 3/5 = **₹36,000** and B gets ₹24,000. (₹30,000 uses the new ratio.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): General Reserve is distributed among the partners in the old profit sharing ratio when the ratio changes.\nReason (R): General Reserve was created out of profits earned when the old ratio was in force.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Past profits belong to the partners in the ratio in which they were earned, which is the old ratio. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Rahul, Sonia and Tarun share profits 4:3:2. From 1 April they agree to share equally. The goodwill of the firm is ₹1,80,000.\nThe entry for goodwill is:", options: ["Sonia's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 To Rahul's Capital A/c ₹20,000", "Tarun's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 To Rahul's Capital A/c ₹20,000", "Rahul's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 To Tarun's Capital A/c ₹20,000", "Tarun's Capital A/c Dr. ₹60,000 To Rahul's Capital A/c ₹60,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Old: 4/9, 3/9, 2/9; new: 3/9 each. Rahul sacrifices 1/9, Sonia is unchanged, Tarun gains 1/9. Tarun pays 1,80,000 × 1/9 = ₹20,000 to Rahul.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 3:2:1 and decide to share 1:2:3 in future. A reserve of ₹1,50,000 is to remain in the books, and goodwill is ignored. The adjusting entry is:", options: ["B's Capital A/c Dr. ₹50,000 To A's Capital A/c ₹50,000", "A's Capital A/c Dr. ₹50,000 To C's Capital A/c ₹50,000", "C's Capital A/c Dr. ₹25,000 To A's Capital A/c ₹25,000", "C's Capital A/c Dr. ₹50,000 To A's Capital A/c ₹50,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Old: 3/6, 2/6, 1/6; new: 1/6, 2/6, 3/6. A sacrifices 2/6, B is unchanged, C gains 2/6. The reserve stays, so C compensates A for 2/6 of it: 1,50,000 × 2/6 = **₹50,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: A, B and C share profits 2:2:1 and agree to share 1:2:2 in future. The goodwill of the firm is ₹1,00,000 (not in the books) and a General Reserve of ₹50,000 is distributed among the partners.\nThe net effect on C's capital account is:", options: ["Decrease of ₹10,000", "Increase of ₹10,000", "Decrease of ₹20,000", "Increase of ₹30,000"], answer: 0, explain: "C gains 2/5 − 1/5 = 1/5 and pays 1,00,000 × 1/5 = ₹20,000 for goodwill. C's share of the reserve = 50,000 × 1/5 = ₹10,000 credited. Net = −20,000 + 10,000 = **decrease of ₹10,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "On a change in profit sharing ratio, the Workmen Compensation Reserve is ₹50,000 and the estimated claim is ₹70,000. The treatment is:", options: ["The reserve is distributed and ₹20,000 is debited to the partners' capital accounts in the new ratio", "The reserve is used against the claim and the shortfall of ₹20,000 is debited to the Revaluation A/c", "₹70,000 is debited to the Revaluation A/c", "The reserve is distributed in the old ratio and the claim is ignored"], answer: 1, explain: "Since the claim exceeds the reserve, nothing is distributed. The reserve is set against the claim and the shortfall of **₹20,000 is debited to the Revaluation A/c** (borne by partners in the old ratio). The liability of ₹70,000 is shown in the balance sheet.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): When the profit sharing ratio changes, the gaining partner's capital account is debited for his share of goodwill.\nReason (R): As per AS 26, the firm raises its self-generated goodwill in the books in such cases.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true: the gainer compensates the sacrificer through the capital accounts. R is false: AS 26 does not allow self-generated goodwill to be raised in the books, so goodwill is dealt with only through the partners' capital accounts.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define the sacrificing ratio.", a: "It is the ratio in which the sacrificing partners give up their share of profit in favour of the gaining partners. Sacrificing ratio = Old ratio − New ratio.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is goodwill not raised in the books of the firm when the profit sharing ratio changes?", a: "Because it is self-generated goodwill, which AS 26 does not allow to be recorded. It is adjusted only through the partners' capital accounts.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits in the ratio 1/2 : 1/3 : 1/6. They decide to share future profits in the ratio 2:1:1. Calculate the sacrificing or gaining ratio.", a: "Old shares: X 1/2, Y 1/3, Z 1/6. New shares: X 2/4 = 1/2, Y 1/4, Z 1/4.\nX: 1/2 − 1/2 = 0 (neither gains nor sacrifices).\nY: 1/3 − 1/4 = 1/12 sacrifice.\nZ: 1/6 − 1/4 = −1/12, a gain of 1/12.\nSo **Y sacrifices 1/12 and Z gains 1/12**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 2:1. They decide to share profits equally in future. Goodwill of the firm is valued at ₹1,20,000. Pass the journal entry.", a: "A: old 2/3, new 1/2, so A sacrifices 1/6. B gains 1/6.\nB pays 1,20,000 × 1/6 = ₹20,000.\nB's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹20,000\n(Being B's gain in goodwill compensated to A)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 3:2:1. They decide to share equally in future. On that date the balance sheet shows: General Reserve ₹90,000; Profit and Loss A/c (debit) ₹30,000; Advertisement Suspense A/c ₹12,000. Pass the journal entries.", a: "These items relate to the old ratio 3:2:1.\n1. General Reserve A/c Dr. ₹90,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹45,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹30,000\n  To C's Capital A/c ₹15,000\n(Being General Reserve distributed in the old ratio)\n2. A's Capital A/c Dr. ₹21,000\n   B's Capital A/c Dr. ₹14,000\n   C's Capital A/c Dr. ₹7,000\n  To Profit and Loss A/c ₹30,000\n  To Advertisement Suspense A/c ₹12,000\n(Being accumulated loss and deferred expenditure written off in the old ratio; total 42,000)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2 and decide to share equally. Stock of ₹80,000 is revalued at ₹70,000, Building of ₹2,00,000 at ₹2,40,000, and a provision for doubtful debts of ₹6,000 is to be created. Prepare the Revaluation Account.", a: "Revaluation A/c:\nDr side: Stock (decrease) ₹10,000; Provision for doubtful debts ₹6,000; Profit transferred to capitals: A ₹12,000, B ₹7,200, C ₹4,800 (total ₹24,000) | Cr side: Building (increase) ₹40,000. (Each side totals ₹40,000.)\nProfit = 40,000 − (10,000 + 6,000) = ₹24,000, shared in the old ratio 5:3:2.\nEntry: Revaluation A/c Dr. ₹24,000 To A's Capital ₹12,000, B's Capital ₹7,200, C's Capital ₹4,800.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2 and decide to share equally. The balance sheet shows Workmen Compensation Reserve ₹60,000 (estimated claim ₹40,000) and Investment Fluctuation Reserve ₹25,000 (investments of book value ₹1,00,000 now have a market value of ₹90,000). Pass the journal entries.", a: "1. Workmen Compensation Reserve A/c Dr. ₹60,000\n  To Workmen Compensation Claim A/c ₹40,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹12,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹8,000\n(Claim of ₹40,000 is provided; the excess ₹20,000 shared 3:2)\n2. Investment Fluctuation Reserve A/c Dr. ₹25,000\n  To Investments A/c ₹10,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹9,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹6,000\n(Fall of ₹10,000 written off; excess ₹15,000 shared 3:2)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2 and have capitals of ₹3,00,000, ₹2,00,000 and ₹1,50,000. From 1 April they decide to share equally. The goodwill of the firm is ₹1,50,000, a General Reserve of ₹60,000 appears in the books and machinery is to be reduced by ₹20,000. Calculate the sacrificing and gaining ratio, pass the goodwill entry and prepare the partners' capital accounts.", a: "Old: 5/10, 3/10, 2/10. New: 1/3 each.\nA: 5/10 − 1/3 = 5/30 sacrifice. B: 3/10 − 1/3 = −1/30, gain 1/30. C: 2/10 − 1/3 = −4/30, gain 4/30.\nGaining ratio B:C = 1:4.\nGoodwill: A's share = 5/30 × 1,50,000 = ₹25,000. B pays ₹5,000 and C pays ₹20,000.\nB's Capital A/c Dr. ₹5,000\nC's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹25,000\nReserve ₹60,000 in 5:3:2: A ₹30,000, B ₹18,000, C ₹12,000. Loss on machinery ₹20,000 in 5:3:2: A ₹10,000, B ₹6,000, C ₹4,000.\n\nA's Capital A/c: Dr side: Revaluation loss ₹10,000; Balance c/d ₹3,45,000 (total ₹3,55,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹3,00,000; B and C (goodwill) ₹25,000; General Reserve ₹30,000 (total ₹3,55,000).\nB's Capital A/c: Dr side: Goodwill ₹5,000; Revaluation loss ₹6,000; Balance c/d ₹2,07,000 (total ₹2,18,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹2,00,000; General Reserve ₹18,000 (total ₹2,18,000).\nC's Capital A/c: Dr side: Goodwill ₹20,000; Revaluation loss ₹4,000; Balance c/d ₹1,38,000 (total ₹1,62,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹1,50,000; General Reserve ₹12,000 (total ₹1,62,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 3:2:1 and decide to share 1:2:3 in future. A General Reserve of ₹90,000 and a credit balance of Profit and Loss A/c of ₹30,000 are to remain in the books, and the goodwill of ₹60,000 is not to appear in the books. Find the sacrificing and gaining ratio and pass a single adjustment entry.", a: "Old: 3/6, 2/6, 1/6. New: 1/6, 2/6, 3/6.\nA: 3/6 − 1/6 = 2/6 sacrifice. B: 2/6 − 2/6 = 0. C: 1/6 − 3/6 = −2/6, a gain of 2/6.\nSo A sacrifices 2/6 and C gains 2/6.\nC's share of goodwill to be paid = 60,000 × 2/6 = ₹20,000.\nC's share of reserves and profit to be paid = (90,000 + 30,000) × 2/6 = ₹40,000.\nTotal = ₹60,000.\nC's Capital A/c Dr. ₹60,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹60,000\n(Being adjustment for goodwill ₹20,000 and reserves and profit ₹40,000 on change in ratio)", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Anita, Bhavna and Charu share profits 5:3:2. From 1 April 2025 they agree to share profits 3:5:2. On that date, the goodwill of the firm is valued at ₹1,50,000 and a General Reserve of ₹90,000 appears in the balance sheet.\n(i) Find the sacrificing and gaining ratio.\n(ii) Pass the entry for goodwill.\n(iii) Pass the entry for the General Reserve.", a: "(i) Old: 5/10, 3/10, 2/10. New: 3/10, 5/10, 2/10. Anita: 5/10 − 3/10 = 2/10 = 1/5 sacrifice. Bhavna: 3/10 − 5/10 = −1/5, a gain of 1/5. Charu: no change. **Anita sacrifices 1/5, Bhavna gains 1/5.**\n(ii) Goodwill: 1,50,000 × 1/5 = ₹30,000.\nBhavna's Capital A/c Dr. ₹30,000\n  To Anita's Capital A/c ₹30,000\n(iii) General Reserve is shared in the old ratio 5:3:2.\nGeneral Reserve A/c Dr. ₹90,000\n  To Anita's Capital A/c ₹45,000\n  To Bhavna's Capital A/c ₹27,000\n  To Charu's Capital A/c ₹18,000", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "admission-of-a-partner": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The premium for goodwill brought in by a new partner is distributed among the old partners in their:", options: ["Old ratio", "New ratio", "Sacrificing ratio", "Capital ratio"], answer: 2, explain: "The premium compensates the old partners for the share of profit they give up, so it is credited to them in the **sacrificing ratio**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On the admission of a new partner, the profit or loss on revaluation of assets and liabilities is shared by:", options: ["All partners including the new partner in the new ratio", "The old partners in the old ratio", "The new partner only", "The old partners in the sacrificing ratio"], answer: 1, explain: "Revaluation relates to the period before admission, so its profit or loss belongs to the **old partners in the old ratio**. The new partner does not share it.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A new partner can be admitted to a firm, unless the deed provides otherwise, only with the consent of:", options: ["The senior-most partner", "The majority of the partners", "All the existing partners", "The Registrar of Firms"], answer: 2, explain: "Section 31 of the Partnership Act requires the **consent of all** the existing partners.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If the premium for goodwill is paid privately by the new partner to the old partners outside the firm, then:", options: ["Premium for Goodwill A/c is credited", "No entry is passed in the books of the firm", "Bank A/c is debited", "The old partners' capital accounts are credited"], answer: 1, explain: "The money does not pass through the firm, so **no entry** is made in the firm's books.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On admission, a new partner acquires which two main rights?", options: ["A share in the assets and a share in the future profits of the firm", "A share in the past profits and in the assets", "A share in the reserves and in the past profits", "The right to manage the firm and to withdraw capital"], answer: 0, explain: "The new partner gets a **share in the assets** of the firm and a **share in its future profits**. He has no share in past profits or reserves.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. C is admitted for a 1/5 share, which he gets from the old partners in their old ratio. The new profit sharing ratio is:", options: ["3:2:1", "12:8:5", "15:10:5", "4:3:1"], answer: 1, explain: "Old partners share the remaining 4/5 in 3:2. A = 3/5 × 4/5 = 12/25; B = 2/5 × 4/5 = 8/25; C = 1/5 = 5/25. New ratio **12:8:5**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. C is admitted for a 1/5 share, which he takes equally from A and B. The new profit sharing ratio is:", options: ["5:3:2", "6:4:3", "3:2:2", "4:3:3"], answer: 0, explain: "Each sacrifices 1/10. A = 3/5 − 1/10 = 1/2; B = 2/5 − 1/10 = 3/10; C = 1/5 = 2/10. New ratio = 5/10 : 3/10 : 2/10 = **5:3:2**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A and B admit C. C brings ₹2,00,000 as capital and ₹60,000 as premium for goodwill, all in cash. The sacrificing ratio of A and B is 2:1. The amount credited to A's capital account as his share of premium is:", options: ["₹30,000", "₹60,000", "₹20,000", "₹40,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Premium is shared in the sacrificing ratio: A = 60,000 × 2/3 = **₹40,000**; B = ₹20,000. The ₹2,00,000 is credited to C's capital account.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The premium for goodwill brought in by a new partner is credited to the old partners' capital accounts.\nReason (R): The old partners give up a part of their share of future profits in favour of the new partner.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Premium is compensation to the old partners for the share they sacrifice, so it is credited to their capital accounts in the sacrificing ratio. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Preeti and Qadir share profits 2:1. Ravi is admitted for a 1/4 share, which he takes from them in the old ratio. His share of goodwill is ₹30,000, but he cannot bring it in cash.\nThe entry for goodwill is:", options: ["Ravi's Current A/c Dr. ₹30,000 To Preeti's Capital A/c ₹18,000 To Qadir's Capital A/c ₹12,000", "Bank A/c Dr. ₹30,000 To Premium for Goodwill A/c ₹30,000", "Ravi's Current A/c Dr. ₹30,000 To Preeti's Capital A/c ₹20,000 To Qadir's Capital A/c ₹10,000", "Preeti's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 Qadir's Capital A/c Dr. ₹10,000 To Ravi's Current A/c ₹30,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Ravi takes his share in the old ratio, so the sacrificing ratio is 2:1. As the premium is not brought in cash, his current (or capital) account is debited and the old partners are credited in the sacrificing ratio: Preeti ₹20,000, Qadir ₹10,000.", level: 2 },
      { q: "C is admitted for a 1/4 share and brings ₹1,50,000 as his capital. The combined adjusted capitals of A and B are ₹3,60,000. The goodwill of the firm is not given. The hidden goodwill of the firm is:", options: ["₹60,000", "₹1,50,000", "₹2,40,000", "₹90,000"], answer: 3, explain: "C's capital for 1/4 share implies a total capital of 1,50,000 × 4 = ₹6,00,000. Actual total capital = 3,60,000 + 1,50,000 = ₹5,10,000. Hidden goodwill = 6,00,000 − 5,10,000 = **₹90,000**. (₹2,40,000 forgets C's capital.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 2:1 and their adjusted capitals are ₹4,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. C is admitted for a 1/4 share. If C's capital is to be in proportion to his share on the basis of the old partners' adjusted capitals, C must bring in:", options: ["₹1,50,000", "₹2,00,000", "₹2,40,000", "₹1,00,000"], answer: 1, explain: "A and B together hold 3/4 of the new firm, so the total capital = 6,00,000 ÷ 3/4 = ₹8,00,000. C's capital = 8,00,000 × 1/4 = **₹2,00,000**. (₹1,50,000 is 1/4 of the old capital.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: Tara and Uma share profits 3:2 and have capitals of ₹3,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. Vimal is admitted for a 1/5 share, getting it from them in the old ratio. He brings ₹1,50,000 as capital and ₹40,000 as premium for goodwill, both in cash. Stock is appreciated by ₹20,000. There are no other adjustments.\nUma's capital account after these adjustments shows a balance of:", options: ["₹2,24,000", "₹2,16,000", "₹2,40,000", "₹2,08,000"], answer: 0, explain: "Sacrificing ratio = old ratio 3:2. Uma's share of premium = 40,000 × 2/5 = ₹16,000. Uma's share of revaluation profit = 20,000 × 2/5 = ₹8,000. Balance = 2,00,000 + 16,000 + 8,000 = **₹2,24,000**.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The profit on revaluation of assets at the time of admission is shared by all the partners, including the new partner.\nReason (R): A Revaluation Account is prepared to record the changes in the values of assets and liabilities when a partner is admitted.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: the revaluation profit or loss belongs to the old partners in the old ratio, because the changes in value arose before the new partner joined. R is true: the Revaluation Account records the changes in values of assets and liabilities.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "If only the new partner's share is given, in what ratio do the old partners sacrifice?", a: "In their **old ratio**, because the old partners share the remaining profit in their old ratio, so each loses the same fraction of his share.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the treatment in the firm's books if the new partner pays his premium for goodwill privately to the old partners.", a: "No entry is passed in the books of the firm, because the amount does not pass through the firm.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2. D is admitted for a 1/5 share, which he gets 3/20 from A and 1/20 from B. Calculate the new profit sharing ratio and the sacrificing ratio.", a: "New share of A = 5/10 − 3/20 = 7/20; B = 3/10 − 1/20 = 5/20; C = 2/10 = 4/20 (unchanged); D = 1/5 = 4/20.\nNew ratio = **7:5:4:4**.\nSacrificing ratio = 3/20 : 1/20 = **3:1** (A and B only).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "P and Q share profits 3:2 (they are also the sacrificing ratio). R is admitted with a capital of ₹3,00,000 and a premium for goodwill of ₹60,000, both brought in cash. Pass the journal entries.", a: "1. Bank A/c Dr. ₹3,60,000\n  To R's Capital A/c ₹3,00,000\n  To Premium for Goodwill A/c ₹60,000\n(Being capital and premium brought in by R)\n2. Premium for Goodwill A/c Dr. ₹60,000\n  To P's Capital A/c ₹36,000\n  To Q's Capital A/c ₹24,000\n(Being premium distributed in the sacrificing ratio 3:2)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. C is admitted for a share which A and B surrender 1/3 and 1/4 of their respective shares, respectively. Calculate the new ratio and the sacrificing ratio. If C brings ₹90,000 as premium for goodwill in cash, pass the entry to distribute it.", a: "A sacrifices 3/5 × 1/3 = 1/5; B sacrifices 2/5 × 1/4 = 1/10. C's share = 1/5 + 1/10 = 3/10.\nNew shares: A = 3/5 − 1/5 = 2/5 = 4/10; B = 2/5 − 1/10 = 3/10; C = 3/10. New ratio = **4:3:3**.\nSacrificing ratio = 1/5 : 1/10 = **2:1**.\nPremium ₹90,000 is shared 2:1: A ₹60,000, B ₹30,000.\nBank A/c Dr. ₹90,000\n  To Premium for Goodwill A/c ₹90,000\nPremium for Goodwill A/c Dr. ₹90,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹60,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹30,000", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. On the admission of C, the following revaluation is agreed: Land of ₹4,00,000 is valued at ₹5,00,000; Machinery of ₹2,00,000 is valued at ₹1,80,000; Creditors of ₹40,000 are to be paid ₹30,000; a Provision for Doubtful Debts of ₹8,000 is to be created. Prepare the Revaluation Account.", a: "Revaluation A/c:\nDr side: Machinery (decrease) ₹20,000; Provision for doubtful debts ₹8,000; Profit transferred to capitals: A ₹49,200, B ₹32,800 (₹82,000) | Cr side: Land (increase) ₹1,00,000; Creditors (decrease in liability) ₹10,000. (Each side totals ₹1,10,000.)\nProfit = 1,10,000 − 28,000 = ₹82,000, shared by A and B in the old ratio 3:2: A = ₹49,200, B = ₹32,800.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 2:1. C is admitted. The balance sheet shows General Reserve ₹60,000, Profit and Loss A/c (credit) ₹30,000 and Advertisement Suspense A/c ₹9,000. Pass the journal entries for these items.", a: "Old partners share these items in the old ratio 2:1.\n1. General Reserve A/c Dr. ₹60,000\n   Profit and Loss A/c Dr. ₹30,000\n  To A's Capital A/c ₹60,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹30,000\n2. A's Capital A/c Dr. ₹6,000\n   B's Capital A/c Dr. ₹3,000\n  To Advertisement Suspense A/c ₹9,000\nNet credit to capitals: A ₹54,000, B ₹27,000 (total ₹81,000 = 90,000 − 9,000).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2 and have capitals of ₹5,00,000 and ₹3,00,000. C is admitted for a 1/5 share, which he takes from A and B in the old ratio. He brings ₹2,00,000 as capital and ₹50,000 as premium for goodwill, both in cash. The balance sheet shows General Reserve ₹40,000. Stock is overvalued by ₹10,000 and is to be written down. Prepare the Revaluation Account and the partners' capital accounts.", a: "Revaluation A/c: Dr side: Stock (decrease) ₹10,000 | Cr side: Loss transferred to A ₹6,000 and B ₹4,000 (₹10,000).\nSacrificing ratio = old ratio 3:2. Premium: A ₹30,000, B ₹20,000. General Reserve: A ₹24,000, B ₹16,000.\n\nA's Capital A/c: Dr side: Revaluation loss ₹6,000; Balance c/d ₹5,48,000 (total ₹5,54,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹5,00,000; General Reserve ₹24,000; Premium ₹30,000 (total ₹5,54,000).\nB's Capital A/c: Dr side: Revaluation loss ₹4,000; Balance c/d ₹3,32,000 (total ₹3,36,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹3,00,000; General Reserve ₹16,000; Premium ₹20,000 (total ₹3,36,000).\nC's Capital A/c: Cr side: Bank ₹2,00,000; Dr side: Balance c/d ₹2,00,000.\nCheck: total capitals = 5,48,000 + 3,32,000 + 2,00,000 = ₹10,80,000 = 8,00,000 + 40,000 − 10,000 + 50,000 + 2,00,000.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. After all adjustments for reserves, revaluation and goodwill, their capitals are A ₹4,50,000 and B ₹2,10,000. C is admitted for a 1/4 share. The new ratio of A, B and C is 9:6:5. C's capital is to be in proportion to his share, and A and B's capitals are to be adjusted in the new ratio on the basis of C's capital. Calculate the new capitals, and show the cash to be brought in or withdrawn.", a: "A and B together hold 3/4 of the firm. Combined adjusted capital of A and B = 4,50,000 + 2,10,000 = ₹6,60,000.\nTotal capital of the new firm = 6,60,000 × 4/3 = ₹8,80,000.\nC's capital = 8,80,000 × 5/20 = **₹2,20,000**.\nA's new capital = 8,80,000 × 9/20 = **₹3,96,000**; B's new capital = 8,80,000 × 6/20 = **₹2,64,000**.\nCheck: 3,96,000 + 2,64,000 + 2,20,000 = ₹8,80,000.\nA: existing ₹4,50,000 − required ₹3,96,000 = ₹54,000 surplus, withdrawn.\nB: required ₹2,64,000 − existing ₹2,10,000 = ₹54,000 shortfall, brought in.\nC brings ₹2,20,000.\nEntries: Bank A/c Dr. ₹2,20,000 To C's Capital A/c ₹2,20,000; Bank A/c Dr. ₹54,000 To B's Capital A/c ₹54,000; A's Capital A/c Dr. ₹54,000 To Bank A/c ₹54,000.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Kirti and Lata share profits 3:1 and their capitals are ₹4,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. Mona is admitted for a 1/4 share in the profits, which she takes from the old partners in the old ratio. She brings ₹3,00,000 as her capital. No value of goodwill is given and the capitals of Kirti and Lata are already adjusted.\n(i) Calculate the goodwill of the firm.\n(ii) Calculate the new profit sharing ratio.\n(iii) Pass the entry for Mona's share of goodwill, which is adjusted through her capital account.", a: "(i) Mona's ₹3,00,000 for a 1/4 share implies a total capital of 3,00,000 × 4 = ₹12,00,000. Actual capital = 6,00,000 + 3,00,000 = ₹9,00,000. Goodwill = 12,00,000 − 9,00,000 = **₹3,00,000**.\n(ii) Mona takes 1/4 in the old ratio 3:1: Kirti sacrifices 3/16, Lata 1/16. New: Kirti = 3/4 − 3/16 = 9/16; Lata = 1/4 − 1/16 = 3/16; Mona = 4/16. New ratio = **9:3:4**.\n(iii) Mona's share of goodwill = 3,00,000 × 1/4 = ₹75,000, shared by Kirti and Lata 3:1.\nMona's Capital A/c Dr. ₹75,000\n  To Kirti's Capital A/c ₹56,250\n  To Lata's Capital A/c ₹18,750", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "retirement-and-death-of-a-partner": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The gaining ratio is calculated as:", options: ["Old ratio − New ratio", "New ratio − Old ratio", "Old ratio ÷ New ratio", "New ratio + Old ratio"], answer: 1, explain: "Gaining ratio = **New share − Old share** of each continuing partner. It shows how the outgoing partner's share is acquired.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On the death of a partner, the amount due to him is transferred to:", options: ["Deceased Partner's Executors A/c", "Realisation A/c", "Revaluation A/c", "The remaining partners' capital A/cs"], answer: 0, explain: "The deceased partner's capital account is closed by transferring the amount due to the **Executors' A/c**, which is paid to the legal representatives.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If no new ratio is mentioned when a partner retires, the continuing partners share future profits in:", options: ["Equal ratio", "Their capital ratio", "The sacrificing ratio", "Their old ratio"], answer: 3, explain: "When nothing is stated, the continuing partners share the retiring partner's share in their **old ratio**, so their gaining ratio equals their old ratio.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The unpaid amount due to a retiring partner that is transferred to his loan account is shown in the balance sheet as:", options: ["An asset", "Capital of the remaining partners", "A deduction from goodwill", "A liability of the firm"], answer: 3, explain: "The retiring partner's loan account is an amount the firm owes to an outsider, so it is a **liability** of the firm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On the retirement of a partner, the profit or loss on revaluation is shared by:", options: ["Continuing partners only, in the gaining ratio", "The retiring partner only", "All partners, including the retiring partner, in the old ratio", "Continuing partners in the new ratio"], answer: 2, explain: "Revaluation arises from the period when the retiring partner was still a partner, so **all partners, including him, share it in the old ratio**.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 5:3:2. C retires, and A and B decide to share future profits 3:2. The gaining ratio of A and B is:", options: ["3:2", "5:3", "1:1", "7:3"], answer: 2, explain: "A gains 3/5 − 5/10 = 1/10 and B gains 2/5 − 3/10 = 1/10. Gaining ratio = **1:1**. Check: total gain 2/10 = C's old share.", level: 2 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits 3:2:1. Z retires and X and Y decide to share equally in future. The goodwill of the firm is ₹2,40,000. The entry for Z's share of goodwill is:", options: ["X's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 Y's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000 To Z's Capital A/c ₹40,000", "Z's Capital A/c Dr. ₹40,000 To Y's Capital A/c ₹40,000", "Y's Capital A/c Dr. ₹80,000 To Z's Capital A/c ₹80,000", "Y's Capital A/c Dr. ₹40,000 To Z's Capital A/c ₹40,000"], answer: 3, explain: "X: new 1/2 − old 3/6 = 0. Y: new 1/2 − old 2/6 = 1/6 gain. Only Y gains, so Y compensates Z for his 1/6 share: 2,40,000 × 1/6 = **₹40,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits in the ratio 2:1:1. The profit of last year was ₹2,40,000. C dies on 31 July and the year ends on 31 March. C's share of profit till his death (time basis, last year's profit) is:", options: ["₹20,000", "₹60,000", "₹15,000", "₹80,000"], answer: 0, explain: "C's share = 1/4. Period = April to July = 4 months. Profit = 2,40,000 × 4/12 × 1/4 = **₹20,000**. (₹60,000 is his full-year share.)", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): On retirement, the continuing partners compensate the retiring partner for his share of goodwill in the gaining ratio.\nReason (R): The continuing partners are the ones who acquire the retiring partner's share of future profits.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The retiring partner's share is taken over by the continuing partners in the gaining ratio, so they pay him for it in the same ratio.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Mr. Rao retired from a firm on 31 March 2025. The amount due to him, ₹4,00,000, is not paid and the continuing partners carry on the business using his money. The partnership deed is silent on the matter.\nUnder Section 37 of the Partnership Act, Mr. Rao can claim:", options: ["Only interest at 6% p.a.", "Only the share of profits earned with the use of his money", "Interest at 6% p.a. or the share of profits earned with his money, at his option", "Nothing, since the deed is silent"], answer: 2, explain: "Section 37 gives the outgoing partner (or his estate) the **option of 6% p.a. interest or the profit earned by using his share**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "On retirement, a partner's capital is ₹3,00,000. His share of goodwill is ₹40,000, of General Reserve ₹20,000 and of revaluation loss ₹5,000. His drawings were ₹15,000. The amount due to him is:", options: ["₹3,55,000", "₹3,60,000", "₹3,40,000", "₹3,20,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Amount due = 3,00,000 + 40,000 + 20,000 − 5,000 − 15,000 = **₹3,40,000**. (₹3,60,000 ignores the loss and drawings.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits equally. Z dies during the year. The sales of the previous year were ₹12,00,000 with a profit of ₹1,80,000. Sales from 1 April to the date of death were ₹6,00,000. Z's share of profit till death on the sales basis is:", options: ["₹60,000", "₹30,000", "₹90,000", "₹15,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Profit rate = 1,80,000 ÷ 12,00,000 = 15%. Profit till death = 6,00,000 × 15% = ₹90,000. Z's 1/3 share = **₹30,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: Gill retires from a firm. The amount due to him is ₹3,60,000. The firm pays ₹1,60,000 in cash and transfers the balance to Gill's Loan A/c, on which interest is payable at 10% p.a.\nThe interest on the loan for the first year is:", options: ["₹36,000", "₹20,000", "₹16,000", "₹10,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Loan = 3,60,000 − 1,60,000 = ₹2,00,000. Interest = 2,00,000 × 10% = **₹20,000**. (₹36,000 is on the whole amount due.)", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Goodwill already appearing in the books at the time of retirement is written off in the gaining ratio.\nReason (R): The gaining ratio is the ratio in which the continuing partners acquire the retiring partner's share.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: existing goodwill is written off among all the partners, including the retiring partner, in the old ratio. R is true: the gaining ratio shows how the continuing partners acquire the retiring partner's share.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is the gaining ratio?", a: "It is the ratio in which the continuing partners gain (acquire) the share of the outgoing partner: Gaining ratio = New ratio − Old ratio.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give the journal entry to transfer the amount due to a deceased partner to his executors.", a: "Deceased Partner's Capital A/c Dr.\n  To Deceased Partner's Executors A/c\n(Being the amount due transferred to the executors)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two ways in which the amount due to a retiring partner may be settled.", a: "(1) Paid in full in cash at once.\n(2) Transferred to his loan account and paid later in instalments with interest. (Or part is paid in cash and the balance transferred to his loan account.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 4:3:2. C retires, and A and B decide to share future profits 3:2. Calculate the gaining ratio.", a: "Old shares: A 4/9, B 3/9, C 2/9. New shares: A 3/5, B 2/5.\nA's gain = 3/5 − 4/9 = (27 − 20)/45 = 7/45.\nB's gain = 2/5 − 3/9 = (18 − 15)/45 = 3/45.\nGaining ratio = 7:3. Check: 10/45 = 2/9 = C's share. ✓", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 3:2:1. B retires, and A and C will share future profits 2:1. The goodwill of the firm is ₹1,20,000. Pass the entry for B's share of goodwill. Also pass the entry if goodwill of ₹60,000 already appears in the books.", a: "A: new 2/3 − old 3/6 = 1/6 gain. C: new 1/3 − old 1/6 = 1/6 gain. Gaining ratio 1:1.\nB's share of goodwill = 1/3 × 1,20,000 = ₹40,000, paid by A and C equally.\nA's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000\nC's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000\n  To B's Capital A/c ₹40,000\nExisting goodwill is written off among all three in the old ratio 3:2:1:\nA's Capital A/c Dr. ₹30,000\nB's Capital A/c Dr. ₹20,000\nC's Capital A/c Dr. ₹10,000\n  To Goodwill A/c ₹60,000", marks: 3 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits equally. Z dies on 1 August 2025. The firm's year ends on 31 March. The profits of the last three years were ₹60,000, ₹90,000 and ₹1,20,000. Calculate Z's share of profit till death on the basis of the average profit of the last three years, and pass the entry.", a: "Average profit = (60,000 + 90,000 + 1,20,000) ÷ 3 = ₹90,000.\nPeriod from 1 April to 31 July = 4 months.\nProfit for 4 months = 90,000 × 4/12 = ₹30,000.\nZ's share = 1/3 × 30,000 = **₹10,000**.\nProfit and Loss Suspense A/c Dr. ₹10,000\n  To Z's Capital A/c ₹10,000\n(Being Z's share of profit till death)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the rights of a retiring partner under Section 37 of the Indian Partnership Act, 1932 when the amount due to him is not paid and the business is continued.", a: "(1) If a partner retires (or dies) and the continuing partners carry on the business without settling his account, he (or his estate) is entitled to a claim on the use of his money.\n(2) The claim is, **at his option**, either interest at 6% p.a. on the amount due to him, or the share of profits earned since his retirement that is attributable to the use of his share of the property.\n(3) This applies unless there is a contract to the contrary, for example a clause in the deed fixing the settlement.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 3:2:1 and their capitals are ₹4,00,000, ₹3,00,000 and ₹2,00,000. C retires. A and B will share profits 3:2 in future. The goodwill of the firm is ₹1,80,000, a General Reserve of ₹60,000 exists, and Land is appreciated by ₹30,000 while Stock is reduced by ₹6,000. C is paid in cash. Prepare the Revaluation Account and the partners' capital accounts.", a: "Revaluation A/c: Dr side: Stock (decrease) ₹6,000; Profit transferred to A ₹12,000, B ₹8,000, C ₹4,000 (₹24,000) | Cr side: Land (increase) ₹30,000. (Each side ₹30,000.)\nGaining ratio: A 3/5 − 3/6 = 1/10; B 2/5 − 2/6 = 1/15. Gaining ratio = 3:2 (3/30 : 2/30), total 5/30 = 1/6 = C's share.\nC's share of goodwill = 1/6 × 1,80,000 = ₹30,000: A pays ₹18,000, B pays ₹12,000.\nGeneral Reserve ₹60,000 in 3:2:1: A ₹30,000, B ₹20,000, C ₹10,000.\n\nA's Capital A/c: Dr side: Goodwill ₹18,000; Balance c/d ₹4,24,000 (total ₹4,42,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹4,00,000; Revaluation profit ₹12,000; General Reserve ₹30,000 (total ₹4,42,000).\nB's Capital A/c: Dr side: Goodwill ₹12,000; Balance c/d ₹3,16,000 (total ₹3,28,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹3,00,000; Revaluation profit ₹8,000; General Reserve ₹20,000 (total ₹3,28,000).\nC's Capital A/c: Dr side: Bank (paid in full) ₹2,44,000 | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹2,00,000; Revaluation profit ₹4,000; General Reserve ₹10,000; Goodwill ₹30,000 (total ₹2,44,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A, B and C share profits 2:2:1 and have capitals of ₹3,00,000, ₹2,00,000 and ₹1,00,000. C dies on 30 June 2025. The firm's year ends on 31 March. A General Reserve of ₹50,000 appears in the books. Goodwill of the firm is valued at ₹1,50,000. C's share of profit till death is to be calculated on the basis of last year's profit of ₹1,20,000. C's drawings till death were ₹10,000. A and B will share profits equally in future. ₹36,000 is paid to C's executors at once and the balance is to be paid later. Prepare C's Capital Account and the Executors' Account.", a: "Profit till death = 1,20,000 × 3/12 × 1/5 = ₹6,000 (P&L Suspense A/c Dr., C's Capital A/c Cr.).\nGeneral Reserve: C's share = 50,000 × 1/5 = ₹10,000.\nGoodwill: C's share = 1,50,000 × 1/5 = ₹30,000. A gains 1/2 − 2/5 = 1/10 and B gains 1/10, so each pays ₹15,000.\nAmount due to C = 1,00,000 + 10,000 + 30,000 + 6,000 − 10,000 = **₹1,36,000**.\n\nC's Capital A/c: Dr side: Drawings ₹10,000; C's Executors A/c ₹1,36,000 (total ₹1,46,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹1,00,000; General Reserve ₹10,000; Goodwill (A and B) ₹30,000; P&L Suspense A/c ₹6,000 (total ₹1,46,000).\nC's Executors A/c: Dr side: Bank ₹36,000; Balance c/d (payable later) ₹1,00,000 (total ₹1,36,000) | Cr side: C's Capital A/c ₹1,36,000 (total ₹1,36,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Rohit, Sana and Tarun share profits 3:2:1. Sana retires on 31 March 2025 and Rohit and Tarun decide to share future profits equally. The goodwill of the firm is valued at ₹90,000. The amount due to Sana is ₹2,40,000; ₹1,00,000 is paid in cash and the balance is transferred to her loan account, to be paid in two equal annual instalments with interest at 9% p.a. on the opening balance.\n(i) Calculate the gaining ratio.\n(ii) Pass the entry for Sana's share of goodwill.\n(iii) Calculate the interest on Sana's loan for the first year.", a: "(i) Rohit: new 1/2 − old 3/6 = 0. Tarun: new 1/2 − old 1/6 = 1/3. Only Tarun gains, so he acquires all of Sana's 1/3 share.\n(ii) Sana's share of goodwill = 90,000 × 1/3 = ₹30,000.\nTarun's Capital A/c Dr. ₹30,000\n  To Sana's Capital A/c ₹30,000\n(iii) Loan = 2,40,000 − 1,00,000 = ₹1,40,000. Interest for the first year = 1,40,000 × 9/100 = **₹12,600**. (Each instalment of principal is ₹70,000.)", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "dissolution-of-partnership-firm": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Dissolution of a firm with the consent of all the partners (Section 40) is called dissolution:", options: ["By notice", "By agreement", "Compulsory", "By the court"], answer: 1, explain: "Section 40 allows a firm to be dissolved **by agreement** among all the partners, or in accordance with a contract between them.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT transferred to the Realisation Account on dissolution?", options: ["Sundry debtors", "Creditors", "Investments", "A partner's loan to the firm"], answer: 3, explain: "A partner's loan is not transferred to Realisation A/c. It is paid directly, after outside liabilities, as per Section 48.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Under Section 48 of the Partnership Act, the assets of the firm on dissolution are first applied to pay:", options: ["Partners' capitals", "Partners' loans", "Debts due to third parties", "The surplus to partners"], answer: 2, explain: "The order is: (1) debts to third parties, (2) partners' loans and advances, (3) partners' capitals, (4) any surplus to partners in the profit sharing ratio.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The profit or loss on realisation is transferred to the partners' capital accounts in:", options: ["Profit sharing ratio", "Capital ratio", "Equal ratio", "Sacrificing ratio"], answer: 0, explain: "Realisation profit or loss belongs to the partners in their **profit sharing ratio** unless the deed says otherwise.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following does NOT result in the dissolution of a firm?", options: ["Retirement of a partner", "Insolvency of all the partners", "Expiry of the fixed term of the partnership", "Completion of the venture for which the firm was formed"], answer: 0, explain: "Retirement of a partner only dissolves the partnership; the firm continues with the other partners. Insolvency of all partners, expiry of the term and completion of the venture dissolve the firm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "On dissolution, assets (other than cash) of book value ₹5,00,000 realised ₹5,30,000. Liabilities of ₹1,00,000 were settled for ₹90,000 and realisation expenses were ₹6,000. The profit on realisation is:", options: ["₹46,000", "₹40,000", "₹34,000", "₹24,000"], answer: 2, explain: "Gain on assets = ₹30,000. Gain on liabilities = 1,00,000 − 90,000 = ₹10,000. Less expenses ₹6,000. Profit = 30,000 + 10,000 − 6,000 = **₹34,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "After selling all assets, a firm has a bank balance of ₹1,00,000. It owes ₹40,000 to outside creditors and ₹35,000 to a partner as loan. The amount left to be paid to partners on account of their capital is:", options: ["₹60,000", "₹25,000", "₹65,000", "₹1,00,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Creditors are paid first (₹40,000), then the partner's loan (₹35,000). The remainder = 1,00,000 − 40,000 − 35,000 = **₹25,000**.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Realisation expenses of ₹5,000 were paid by a partner on behalf of the firm. The entry is:", options: ["Partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Realisation A/c", "Realisation A/c Dr. To Bank A/c", "Realisation A/c Dr. To Partner's Loan A/c", "Realisation A/c Dr. To Partner's Capital A/c"], answer: 3, explain: "The expense is a cost of realisation, so Realisation A/c is debited. The partner paid it from his own pocket, so his **capital account is credited**.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): On dissolution, a partner's loan to the firm is paid before the partners' capitals are repaid.\nReason (R): Section 48 requires partners' loans and advances to be paid after the outside debts and before the partners' capitals.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The statutory order of payment is outsiders, then partners' loans, then capitals. R states that order and so explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A firm is being dissolved. An old computer, fully written off in earlier years and so not in the books, is sold for ₹6,000.\nThe entry for the sale is:", options: ["Realisation A/c Dr. ₹6,000 To Bank A/c ₹6,000", "Bank A/c Dr. ₹6,000 To Computer A/c ₹6,000", "Bank A/c Dr. ₹6,000 To Realisation A/c ₹6,000", "Bank A/c Dr. ₹6,000 To Partners' Capital A/cs ₹6,000"], answer: 2, explain: "The computer has no book value, so there is no asset account to credit. The amount realised is credited to **Realisation A/c** (as a gain on realisation): Bank A/c Dr. → To Realisation A/c.", level: 2 },
      { q: "On dissolution, assets (other than cash) of ₹7,00,000 include stock of ₹60,000, which a partner takes over at ₹55,000. The other assets of book value ₹6,40,000 realise ₹5,90,000. An unrecorded investment is sold for ₹12,000. Creditors of ₹1,20,000 are settled for ₹1,15,000 and realisation expenses of ₹8,000 are paid by the firm. The loss on realisation is:", options: ["₹41,000", "₹46,000", "₹51,000", "₹53,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Debit side: assets 7,00,000 + creditors paid 1,15,000 + expenses 8,000 = ₹8,23,000. Credit side: creditors 1,20,000 + stock taken over 55,000 + assets sold 5,90,000 + investment 12,000 = ₹7,77,000. Loss = 8,23,000 − 7,77,000 = **₹46,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "P, Q and R share profits 3:2:1. Their capitals are ₹2,00,000, ₹1,00,000 and ₹60,000. A General Reserve of ₹60,000 exists and the realisation loss is ₹90,000. Nothing else remains to be adjusted. The amount finally paid to Q is:", options: ["₹70,000", "₹1,20,000", "₹1,00,000", "₹90,000"], answer: 3, explain: "Q's share of reserve = 60,000 × 2/6 = ₹20,000 (credit). Q's share of loss = 90,000 × 2/6 = ₹30,000 (debit). Q receives 1,00,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 = **₹90,000**.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Case: X and Y share profits equally. At dissolution their capital accounts are X ₹1,00,000 (Cr.) and Y ₹40,000 (Cr.). The loss on realisation is ₹1,20,000 and there are no other items left.\nWhat must Y do and what is paid to X?", options: ["Y brings ₹60,000; X is paid ₹40,000", "Y brings ₹20,000; X is paid ₹40,000", "Y brings ₹20,000; X is paid ₹1,00,000", "Y brings no cash; X is paid ₹40,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Each bears a loss of ₹60,000. X's capital = 1,00,000 − 60,000 = ₹40,000 (credit, paid to him). Y's capital = 40,000 − 60,000 = −₹20,000, a debit balance (deficiency), which **Y must bring in**. Cash from the realisation covers the rest.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A partner's loan to the firm is transferred to the Realisation Account on dissolution.\nReason (R): The Realisation Account records the sale of assets and the settlement of the firm's outside liabilities.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: a partner's loan is not an outside liability and is paid directly through the bank account after outsiders are paid. R is true: Realisation A/c deals with assets and outside liabilities only.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Give the journal entry for a machine taken over by a partner on dissolution at an agreed value of ₹25,000.", a: "Partner's Capital A/c Dr. ₹25,000\n  To Realisation A/c ₹25,000\n(Being machine taken over by the partner)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Pass the entry for realisation expenses of ₹4,000 paid by partner Ajay on behalf of the firm.", a: "Realisation A/c Dr. ₹4,000\n  To Ajay's Capital A/c ₹4,000\n(Being realisation expenses paid by Ajay)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two circumstances in which a firm is dissolved (a) compulsorily or (b) on the happening of certain contingencies.", a: "(1) **Compulsory dissolution (Section 41)**: when all the partners, or all but one, are declared insolvent, or when the business becomes unlawful.\n(2) **On the happening of contingencies (Section 42)**: expiry of the fixed term, completion of the venture, or death or insolvency of a partner (unless the deed says otherwise).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Pass journal entries on the dissolution of a firm for: (a) creditors of ₹30,000 (book value) settled by paying ₹28,000; (b) a bank loan of ₹50,000 taken over by partner Mehra at its book value.", a: "(a) Realisation A/c Dr. ₹28,000\n  To Bank A/c ₹28,000\n(Being creditors paid ₹28,000 in full settlement; creditors were transferred to Realisation A/c at ₹30,000, giving a gain of ₹2,000)\n(b) Realisation A/c Dr. ₹50,000\n  To Mehra's Capital A/c ₹50,000\n(Being bank loan taken over by Mehra)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A and B share profits 3:2. On dissolution, assets (other than cash) of book value ₹4,50,000 realised ₹4,80,000. Creditors of ₹1,00,000 were paid ₹96,000 in full settlement and realisation expenses of ₹5,000 were paid by the firm. Prepare the Realisation Account.", a: "Realisation A/c:\nDr side: Sundry assets ₹4,50,000; Bank (creditors paid) ₹96,000; Bank (expenses) ₹5,000; Profit transferred: A ₹17,400, B ₹11,600 (₹29,000) — total ₹5,80,000 | Cr side: Creditors ₹1,00,000; Bank (assets realised) ₹4,80,000 — total ₹5,80,000.\nProfit = Credit side 5,80,000 − Debit side 5,51,000 = ₹29,000. A = 29,000 × 3/5 = ₹17,400; B = ₹11,600.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "X, Y and Z share profits 3:2:1. On dissolution their capital balances (all credit) after other adjustments are X ₹90,000, Y ₹60,000 and Z ₹15,000. The loss on realisation is ₹1,20,000. Show the partners' capital accounts and the amount Z must bring in.", a: "Loss shared 3:2:1: X ₹60,000, Y ₹40,000, Z ₹20,000.\nX's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation loss ₹60,000; Bank (paid) ₹30,000 (total ₹90,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹90,000.\nY's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation loss ₹40,000; Bank (paid) ₹20,000 (total ₹60,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹60,000.\nZ's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation loss ₹20,000 (total ₹20,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹15,000; Bank (cash brought in) ₹5,000 (total ₹20,000).\nZ's account shows a deficiency of 15,000 − 20,000 = ₹5,000, which **Z must bring in**. Check: cash paid out 30,000 + 20,000 = 50,000 = cash left after realisation 45,000 + 5,000 brought by Z.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Pass journal entries on the dissolution of a firm for: (a) unrecorded investments of ₹9,000 sold for ₹11,000; (b) an unrecorded liability of ₹3,000 settled for ₹2,500; (c) realisation expenses of ₹2,000 paid by partner R.", a: "(a) Bank A/c Dr. ₹11,000\n  To Realisation A/c ₹11,000\n(Being unrecorded investments sold)\n(b) Realisation A/c Dr. ₹2,500\n  To Bank A/c ₹2,500\n(Being unrecorded liability paid)\n(c) Realisation A/c Dr. ₹2,000\n  To R's Capital A/c ₹2,000\n(Being realisation expenses paid by R)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A and B share profits equally. Their balance sheet on 31 March 2025 showed: Capital A ₹1,50,000, B ₹1,00,000; General Reserve ₹40,000; Creditors ₹60,000; B's Loan ₹20,000. Assets: Land ₹80,000; Machinery ₹1,20,000; Stock ₹80,000; Debtors ₹70,000; Cash ₹20,000. The firm was dissolved. Land realised ₹1,00,000, Machinery ₹1,00,000, Stock ₹70,000 and Debtors ₹60,000. Creditors were paid ₹58,000 in full settlement and realisation expenses were ₹4,000. Prepare the Realisation Account, the partners' capital accounts and the Bank Account.", a: "Realisation A/c:\nDr side: Land ₹80,000; Machinery ₹1,20,000; Stock ₹80,000; Debtors ₹70,000; Bank (creditors) ₹58,000; Bank (expenses) ₹4,000 (total ₹4,12,000) | Cr side: Creditors ₹60,000; Bank (assets realised) ₹3,30,000; Loss: A ₹11,000, B ₹11,000 (total ₹4,12,000).\nLoss = 4,12,000 − 3,90,000 = ₹22,000.\n\nA's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation loss ₹11,000; Bank ₹1,59,000 (total ₹1,70,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹1,50,000; General Reserve ₹20,000 (total ₹1,70,000).\nB's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation loss ₹11,000; Bank ₹1,09,000 (total ₹1,20,000) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹1,00,000; General Reserve ₹20,000 (total ₹1,20,000).\n\nBank A/c: Dr side: Balance b/d ₹20,000; Realisation (assets realised) ₹3,30,000 (total ₹3,50,000) | Cr side: Realisation (creditors) ₹58,000; Realisation (expenses) ₹4,000; B's Loan ₹20,000; A's Capital ₹1,59,000; B's Capital ₹1,09,000 (total ₹3,50,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "X and Y share profits 3:2. Their balance sheet shows: Capital X ₹2,00,000, Y ₹1,00,000; Creditors ₹80,000; Y's Loan ₹30,000. Assets: Land ₹1,50,000; Stock ₹1,20,000; Debtors ₹90,000; Cash ₹50,000. On dissolution: Land realised ₹1,80,000; Stock was taken over by X at ₹1,00,000; Debtors realised ₹80,000; an unrecorded typewriter was sold for ₹6,000; Creditors were paid ₹78,000; realisation expenses ₹4,000 were paid by the firm. Prepare the Realisation Account, the partners' capital accounts and the Bank Account.", a: "Realisation A/c:\nDr side: Land ₹1,50,000; Stock ₹1,20,000; Debtors ₹90,000; Bank (creditors) ₹78,000; Bank (expenses) ₹4,000; Profit: X ₹2,400, Y ₹1,600 (₹4,000) — total ₹4,46,000 | Cr side: Creditors ₹80,000; Bank (land) ₹1,80,000; X's Capital (stock) ₹1,00,000; Bank (debtors) ₹80,000; Bank (typewriter) ₹6,000 — total ₹4,46,000.\n\nX's Capital A/c: Dr side: Realisation (stock taken) ₹1,00,000; Bank ₹1,02,400 (total ₹2,02,400) | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹2,00,000; Realisation profit ₹2,400 (total ₹2,02,400).\nY's Capital A/c: Dr side: Bank ₹1,01,600 | Cr side: Balance b/d ₹1,00,000; Realisation profit ₹1,600 (total ₹1,01,600).\n\nBank A/c: Dr side: Balance b/d ₹50,000; Realisation (land) ₹1,80,000; Realisation (debtors) ₹80,000; Realisation (typewriter) ₹6,000 (total ₹3,16,000) | Cr side: Realisation (creditors) ₹78,000; Realisation (expenses) ₹4,000; Y's Loan ₹30,000; X's Capital ₹1,02,400; Y's Capital ₹1,01,600 (total ₹3,16,000).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Dev and Eka share profits 3:2. On 31 March 2025 the firm is dissolved. Assets (other than cash) of book value ₹3,00,000 include a machine of book value ₹40,000, which Eka takes over at ₹35,000. The remaining assets realise ₹2,40,000. Creditors of ₹80,000 are paid ₹76,000 in full settlement. Realisation expenses of ₹5,000 are paid by Dev.\n(i) Pass the entries for the machine and for the expenses.\n(ii) Find the total of the credit side of the Realisation A/c before the loss is transferred.\n(iii) Calculate the loss on realisation and each partner's share.", a: "(i) Eka's Capital A/c Dr. ₹35,000\n  To Realisation A/c ₹35,000\nRealisation A/c Dr. ₹5,000\n  To Dev's Capital A/c ₹5,000\n(ii) Credit side: Creditors ₹80,000 + Bank (assets realised) ₹2,40,000 + Eka's Capital (machine) ₹35,000 = **₹3,55,000**.\n(iii) Debit side: Assets ₹3,00,000 + Bank (creditors) ₹76,000 + Dev's Capital (expenses) ₹5,000 = ₹3,81,000.\nLoss = 3,81,000 − 3,55,000 = **₹26,000**. Dev's share = 26,000 × 3/5 = ₹15,600; Eka's share = 26,000 × 2/5 = ₹10,400.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
